WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:04.857
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.857 --> 00:00:14.587
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I love that you guys have to stay here.

00:00:14.587 --> 00:00:16.181
So I really don't have to be that funny.

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:21.417
It's like you cannot leave the room and
I did enjoy meeting John.

00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:25.074
And in the back before we started, I just
have to say, he asked me what my husband

00:00:25.074 --> 00:00:28.292
did and somehow the conversation came
out that he used to be a rapper.

00:00:28.292 --> 00:00:34.093
[LAUGH] And then I made the mistake,
I said, yeah.

00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:37.240
He was most known for two songs,
Shake It Like A White Girl and

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.230
College Girls Are Easy.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.860
&gt;&gt; And I was like that probably wasn't

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.326
the right thing to say to the dean

00:00:43.326 --> 00:00:44.094
of the school.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:44.094 --> 00:00:45.741
&gt;&gt; So I was like never mind, never mind.

00:00:45.741 --> 00:00:52.904
Anyway, I'm happy to be here.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:52.904 --> 00:00:54.392
&gt;&gt; So Sarah, thanks for being here.

00:00:54.392 --> 00:00:54.911
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:00:54.911 --> 00:00:56.548
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:56.548 --> 00:00:57.291
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me.

00:00:57.291 --> 00:00:59.030
&gt;&gt; Clearly, we're excited to have you.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.249
I'm particularly excited to have you here.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:04.080
I never thought I'd be saying this
to a view from the top speaker.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.265
But if it weren't for you, I don't think
I would have been able to zip up these

00:01:07.265 --> 00:01:09.209
pants today.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:09.209 --> 00:01:09.751
&gt;&gt; So-

00:01:09.751 --> 00:01:10.474
&gt;&gt; I'm so honored.

00:01:10.474 --> 00:01:12.762
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:12.762 --> 00:01:13.594
&gt;&gt; So really, thank you.

00:01:13.594 --> 00:01:17.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.211
&gt;&gt; You famously started Spanx with $5,000

00:01:20.211 --> 00:01:21.580
in your bank account.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.401
What was the hardest part about getting

00:01:24.401 --> 00:01:28.097
Spanx off the ground?
&gt;&gt; The hardest part about getting Spanx

00:01:28.097 --> 00:01:32.297
off the ground was definitely in the
beginning when you first have an idea as

00:01:32.297 --> 00:01:36.298
an inventor and you've thought of
something that doesn't exist, and

00:01:36.298 --> 00:01:38.247
you want to bring it into the world.

00:01:38.247 --> 00:01:39.680
There were two things that were happening.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.027
One was my own self-doubt was a really
hard part of the journey and then

00:01:44.027 --> 00:01:48.594
the beginning of all the manufacturers
that I was having to cold call, and

00:01:48.594 --> 00:01:51.776
talked to to try to get my
product made were all men.

00:01:51.776 --> 00:01:54.420
And it was much harder to explain and

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.460
to try to get them to understand
the concept of what the product was.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.531
So I heard the word no for
two straight years trying to get it made,

00:02:03.531 --> 00:02:08.492
which then increases the self-doubt that
can happen and one of the interesting

00:02:08.492 --> 00:02:12.511
things about being an inventor is
you don't go to school for it.

00:02:12.511 --> 00:02:13.878
There isn't an inventor class.

00:02:13.878 --> 00:02:16.073
You can't major in inventing.

00:02:16.073 --> 00:02:19.775
And so it's really a belief
system in yourself and

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:24.359
the willingness to look stupid or
to have people laugh at you or

00:02:24.359 --> 00:02:29.940
to fail at something that you believe,
you're your own gut check on that.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.923
And so it takes a lot of confidence.

00:02:31.923 --> 00:02:34.080
So that was the hardest
part of my journey.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.544
There was a lot of starting and
stopping, I'm like am I crazy?

00:02:36.544 --> 00:02:41.419
And everyone's saying no, but
I kept picking myself back

00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:43.713
up to pursue it.
&gt;&gt; What was it?

00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:45.920
Was it confidence that kept
you going in the early days?

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.389
What was it that kept you coming back?
&gt;&gt; Well, my life was pretty sucked.

00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:52.703
So-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:52.703 --> 00:02:53.376
&gt;&gt; That always helps.

00:02:53.376 --> 00:02:54.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.486
&gt;&gt; I was selling fax machines door to door

00:02:57.486 --> 00:02:59.550
for seven years.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.390
And as mentioned, this wasn't mentioned,
but I had wanted to be a lawyer.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.110
My father was a trial attorney and
I used to actually ask to get out of

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.300
school growing up to watch
him in closing arguments, and

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.310
I debated all through high school.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:17.700
I debated in college and
I'm basically a really bad test taker.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.031
And so I bond the LSAT and
I bond it not once, but twice.

00:03:23.031 --> 00:03:27.867
And so it set my life on a different
course for, I would say things like that

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:32.781
happened, then you end up like those
moments in your life where you think,

00:03:32.781 --> 00:03:37.080
I can't believe this is
happening to me is often times.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.818
Life's way of nudging you and
just letting you know you are off course.

00:03:40.818 --> 00:03:42.739
&gt;&gt; Well, thank goodness, you persisted.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:44.099
because today, we have Spanx.

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:48.060
And today, Spanx has moved far
beyond the world of undergarments.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.593
At different points in
the company's history,

00:03:50.593 --> 00:03:53.630
you've both taken on and
walked away from the role of CEO.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:57.118
What has driven those shift?
&gt;&gt; Well, in the early days, I mean,

00:03:57.118 --> 00:03:59.113
as an entrepreneur,
you're every department.

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:02.200
So I was the before and after butt model.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.624
Literally, I took
a picture of my own butt.

00:04:04.624 --> 00:04:08.240
Took it to Kinko's, got it laminated
with and without Spanx on.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.588
Stood in the stores and I'm like hey,
look what this can do for you.

00:04:11.588 --> 00:04:12.196
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:12.196 --> 00:04:14.430
&gt;&gt; I was like the packer and shipper.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.431
I was the head of sales, I was everything.

00:04:16.431 --> 00:04:19.726
And you learn very quickly what you're
good at and what you're not good at, and

00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:21.544
what you enjoy, and what you don't enjoy,

00:04:21.544 --> 00:04:24.320
and there's usually
a correlation between the two.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.444
And so I often say, as soon as you
can afford to, hire your weakness.

00:04:28.444 --> 00:04:32.127
And a few years into Spanx, it was
abundantly clear that the lane that I

00:04:32.127 --> 00:04:35.190
needed to stay in was inventing,
selling, promoting.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.592
Spanx actually has never advertised.

00:04:37.592 --> 00:04:43.360
Spanx is 18 years old and the first
time we've ever sampled an ad was in 16.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.365
And so I was sort of the advertisement and
me being out in the field talking

00:04:48.365 --> 00:04:53.631
about the product and sharing the story
of the why, why we're making this?

00:04:53.631 --> 00:04:57.070
Why it's better was a big
part of the formula?

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:02.273
And I needed someone to be in the company
day-to-day running the daily operations.

00:05:02.273 --> 00:05:06.025
So I hired a CEO,
she was a co-CEO with me for a while and

00:05:06.025 --> 00:05:10.678
then she became the full-time CEO and
she was that for 12 years and

00:05:10.678 --> 00:05:14.270
then I stepped back in as
CEO about two years ago.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.648
It's been two years and
a lot of that has to do with timing.

00:05:17.648 --> 00:05:19.880
I have four children
under the age of eight.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.468
And so I was growing and
building my family and it

00:05:22.468 --> 00:05:27.020
just organically feels like the right time
for me to do that inside of the company.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.960
And so I still am able to stay in my lane,
it's just slightly structured differently

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.986
where I have a very strong management
team around me running the day-to-day

00:05:35.986 --> 00:05:40.538
operations.
&gt;&gt; You still hold 100% ownership over

00:05:40.538 --> 00:05:45.319
Spanx and you made a conscious decision to
never take money from outside investors.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:47.870
Why?
&gt;&gt; I never needed to.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:51.506
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:51.506 --> 00:05:54.176
&gt;&gt; I mean, I just I never needed to, so

00:05:54.176 --> 00:05:59.071
I don't know if it was some super
conscious decision along the way

00:05:59.071 --> 00:06:02.605
more than just I didn't
really have the need.

00:06:02.605 --> 00:06:07.725
Spanx was profitable from the first
month that I was in business and

00:06:07.725 --> 00:06:12.389
I'm of the belief system for
me in my journey to start small,

00:06:12.389 --> 00:06:17.980
think big and scale fast and a lot of
people want to start big and think big.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:21.286
And oftentimes,
get ahead of themselves and

00:06:21.286 --> 00:06:26.822
that can end wildly successfully, but
it can also cause a lot of problems.

00:06:26.822 --> 00:06:28.624
And you dilute yourself down a lot and

00:06:28.624 --> 00:06:31.662
then you have a lot of other
people that you're answering to.

00:06:31.662 --> 00:06:35.350
So that just was the journey that I took.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.700
I mean, I started it with five grand.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:39.784
I've never had any outside funding.

00:06:39.784 --> 00:06:42.420
And whatever money I
made from selling Spanx,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:47.000
I just put back into the business.
&gt;&gt; I love that, start small, think big.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:52.367
Do you want to run Spanx forever?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I operate off gut and

00:06:52.367 --> 00:06:59.650
intuition and I think when
the time is right, I'll know it.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:02.356
When I first started Spax,
I've never taken a business class.

00:07:02.356 --> 00:07:06.450
I've never worked in fashion or
retail when I started Spanx.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:10.590
And about six months after I started it,
all these people kept coming up to me at

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.303
business events or
cocktail parties or wherever.

00:07:13.303 --> 00:07:16.070
And said, Sarah,
what's your exit strategy?

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.634
I was like what are you talking about?

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:21.840
I didn't even know what that meant.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.236
And so eventually, I started telling
people, my exit strategy is that I want to

00:07:25.236 --> 00:07:27.332
exit a room and look good.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:27.332 --> 00:07:28.350
&gt;&gt; That's my exit strategy.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.441
So.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:07:30.441 --> 00:07:35.525
&gt;&gt; The best exit strategy ever.

00:07:35.525 --> 00:07:36.874
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:07:36.874 --> 00:07:37.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.581
&gt;&gt; You mentioned that you're an inventor

00:07:39.581 --> 00:07:41.642
first, many aspiring entrepreneurs,

00:07:41.642 --> 00:07:44.443
even in this room struggle
to find their initial idea.

00:07:44.443 --> 00:07:48.115
How do you constantly find ways
to invent new products and

00:07:48.115 --> 00:07:54.150
where does your inspiration come from?
&gt;&gt; I I mean, I

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.977
think of a lot of ideas at traffic lights.
&gt;&gt; I think of them-

00:07:56.977 --> 00:07:57.992
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:57.992 --> 00:08:00.008
&gt;&gt; All the time in different places, and

00:08:00.008 --> 00:08:02.781
I think it's part of just
being hyper-observant.

00:08:02.781 --> 00:08:06.484
I like to find white space.

00:08:06.484 --> 00:08:09.849
I pay attention to what are things
that haven't evolved and why?

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:15.870
There's certain things in our society that
updates itself and changes constantly,

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.870
and then there's certain
things that's not.

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:21.218
And so I'll ask myself
questions all day everyday.

00:08:21.218 --> 00:08:22.460
It's just the way my brain works.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.860
I could even be looking at a table and
be like, why is the table like that?

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.490
When was the table first created?

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:31.500
Is that the actual best design for a table
or could there be something different?

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.460
And Spanx went into men's,

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:38.370
and randomly how that happened was
because I got curious that the man's

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:43.060
undershirt was created in 1918 and
no one had paid any attention to it since.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:45.220
It was literally the same thing.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:49.383
And so I just talked to my brother and
my husband about it and their undershirts,

00:08:49.383 --> 00:08:53.005
the necks stretched out, or
they were boxy and bulky under clothes.

00:08:53.005 --> 00:08:55.671
And I thought, I'm just going to add
a little bit of lycra to the cotton

00:08:55.671 --> 00:08:57.796
undershirt for men, and so
the neck won't stretch out.

00:08:57.796 --> 00:08:59.763
And I'm going to taper it in at the waist.

00:08:59.763 --> 00:09:02.060
So ideas come to me like that.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:04.650
Our latest invention is arm tights.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:07.540
And ladies, do you want to stand?

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:08.890
Anyone wearing an arm tight?

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:11.560
They just showed me these,
I was very excited.

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:12.647
Stand up.
Okay, here we go.

00:09:12.647 --> 00:09:15.215
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:15.215 --> 00:09:17.354
&gt;&gt; So Jennifer and Naomi have them and

00:09:17.354 --> 00:09:20.904
I feel like there was somebody,
yeah, you have them.

00:09:20.904 --> 00:09:22.281
She's wearing them.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:22.281 --> 00:09:22.856
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:09:22.856 --> 00:09:23.499
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:09:23.499 --> 00:09:24.144
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:09:24.144 --> 00:09:24.969
&gt;&gt; Hello, hello, hello.

00:09:24.969 --> 00:09:25.761
&gt;&gt; I'm hiding them under the blazer.

00:09:25.761 --> 00:09:27.111
&gt;&gt; Well, you put that cute jacket on over,

00:09:27.111 --> 00:09:28.292
that's why.
&gt;&gt; Yes, it's true.

00:09:28.292 --> 00:09:29.902
&gt;&gt; Anyway so the invention, the idea for

00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:32.063
is arm tights, is literally,
I mean, there's so

00:09:32.063 --> 00:09:34.580
much that comes from being
a frustrated consumer.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.610
So I'm a woman, I'm looking at my closet.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:40.032
Most of my favorite things are sleeveless,
like a sleeveless shirt,

00:09:40.032 --> 00:09:41.083
a sleeveless dress.

00:09:41.083 --> 00:09:43.267
And I'm like, you know what,
I want to wear that differently.

00:09:43.267 --> 00:09:45.260
And I also want to wear it year round.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:48.260
So I just want something that
goes underneath, on my arms,

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.328
super simple in lots of different colors.

00:09:50.328 --> 00:09:54.306
I don't want to always have to put a
cardigan or a jacket or something over it,

00:09:54.306 --> 00:09:57.539
come fall or winter or
when you're transitioning in spring or

00:09:57.539 --> 00:09:59.356
even inside of office buildings.

00:09:59.356 --> 00:10:02.870
I'm cold a lot and
I don't want the integrity of my shirt or

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.470
my dress to be covered up.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:05.427
So that's how arm tights.

00:10:05.427 --> 00:10:08.415
It's a little crop top made
on a hosiery tights machine,

00:10:08.415 --> 00:10:11.910
which had never been done before,
and you put it on over your head.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.410
It stops just below your bra and
the reason for

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.290
that is less bulk under your clothes.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.420
It's super breathable, easy to wear.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.338
And then it just literally,
with one $30 item,

00:10:21.338 --> 00:10:25.740
your whole closet exponentially grows with
looks and how you want to layer it and

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.517
wear it.
&gt;&gt; I love it.

00:10:27.517 --> 00:10:29.280
&gt;&gt; But yeah, ideas are-

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:30.822
&gt;&gt; Clearly I love it.

00:10:30.822 --> 00:10:33.950
&gt;&gt; My assistant, who's my right hand Lisa,

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:39.730
she's been my right hand for 16 years,
can attest to this, but I have 99 pages,

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:43.155
I don't know why it's 99, but
she told me the other day it's 99 pages,

00:10:43.155 --> 00:10:47.161
single-spaced, typed, of ideas.
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] one last [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:47.161 --> 00:10:48.882
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:10:48.882 --> 00:10:49.986
&gt;&gt; I need one more idea.

00:10:49.986 --> 00:10:54.985
So I think of ideas constantly,
on airplanes, in car, talking to somebody,

00:10:54.985 --> 00:10:56.597
and I email it to myself.

00:10:56.597 --> 00:11:01.399
And then I just keep them and log them.
&gt;&gt; Wow, [LAUGH]

00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:03.400
take note.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.210
You've said that you deliberately
approached Spanx with a degree of

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:08.390
feminism you had rarely seen in business.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:10.869
Can you elaborate on that for us?
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:11:10.869 --> 00:11:16.118
I approached it with a very
feminine leadership style.

00:11:16.118 --> 00:11:21.284
And so
I think that traditional business has been

00:11:21.284 --> 00:11:27.086
very masculine and
it's been a very masculine model.

00:11:27.086 --> 00:11:31.194
And so I approach it, I mean, and when
I say that, when I first started Spanx,

00:11:31.194 --> 00:11:35.307
I was maybe three months in and I was at
a cocktail party and these three guys came

00:11:35.307 --> 00:11:39.320
up to me and they said, Sarah, so
we heard you invented something.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.370
And I said, yes, I did,
and they said, great.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:46.250
And one guy pat me on the shoulder and
he said, I hope you're ready to go to war,

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.410
and he said, business is war.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:54.340
And I just remember looking at him and
thinking, why?

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:58.370
And I went home that night and
sat in my apartment.

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:02.980
And I sat on the floor and I was thinking
about all that was happening and

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:07.000
this risk I was taking in
potentially leaving my job and

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.870
the secure income and just thinking,
I don't want to go to war.

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:13.737
And this voice inside of my head
just said, do it different,

00:12:13.737 --> 00:12:15.493
take a totally different approach.

00:12:15.493 --> 00:12:19.719
And so I have approached Spanx
with very feminine principles.

00:12:19.719 --> 00:12:24.810
And there's the feminine and the masculine
in all of us and it's all super important.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:29.827
But using vulnerability,
really operating off of intuition.

00:12:29.827 --> 00:12:36.220
If the data Is trumping my intuition,
I go with my intuition almost every time.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:41.030
So that's not typical in
a corporate environment.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:46.180
So those kinds of things that I've done,
like being vulnerable with the business.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:49.950
I felt as a consumer, I just felt like

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:54.195
companies aren't really talking to me
the way that I want them to talk to me.

00:12:54.195 --> 00:12:56.080
And I'm not listening to them,
I don't believe them.

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:57.647
I don't necessarily trust them.

00:12:57.647 --> 00:13:04.510
And so when I started Spanx,
instead of talking at my customer,

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:10.335
I wanted to talk to them and
I made myself vulnerable.

00:13:10.335 --> 00:13:14.610
So I joked and said I used my own
butt in the before and after picture.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:18.360
But I had felt like companies
were operating in this like,

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:22.090
we need to be perfect and
you need to see us as the authority and

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:25.580
you need me, and
that's how I'm going to sell you product.

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.030
And I was like, hey, I'm one of you.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:30.440
Here's what's happening.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:31.950
Here's what it does for me.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.570
This is why it works.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.780
And it was just a very different approach.

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.674
And I felt consumers became really
connected and really loyal.

00:13:39.674 --> 00:13:44.862
And probably part of the reason why Spanx
as a brand didn't need to advertise,

00:13:44.862 --> 00:13:48.713
we haven't spent any money
on advertising in 16 years,

00:13:48.713 --> 00:13:53.980
because a lot of it is word of mouth
from women sharing with other women.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah, if that flashing example in

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:57.140
the green room we had earlier-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.110
&gt;&gt; Was any example of what that virality

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:00.830
looks like for
you then I can only imagine.

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.557
Can you tell us about
a meaningful experience-

00:14:03.557 --> 00:14:04.775
&gt;&gt; I tried to get John to flash, but

00:14:04.775 --> 00:14:05.957
he wasn't wearing any Spanx.

00:14:05.957 --> 00:14:15.957
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:17.968 --> 00:14:18.829
&gt;&gt; Moving on from that.

00:14:18.829 --> 00:14:22.478
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:22.478 --> 00:14:24.305
&gt;&gt; Can you tell us about a meaningful

00:14:24.305 --> 00:14:28.190
experience you've had mentoring
young women since founding Spanx?

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:29.992
&gt;&gt; I mean, sure,

00:14:29.992 --> 00:14:34.444
a lot of different women come to mind.

00:14:34.444 --> 00:14:39.327
I look for women that I can support
what they're doing in education and

00:14:39.327 --> 00:14:42.480
in their business as an entrepreneurship.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.660
There's a group of ten women
that I started supporting and

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:49.320
mentoring in Atlanta, I wanted to kind of
do something really close to home,

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:54.300
who are all doing social impact-type
businesses for the community of Atlanta.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.000
And that's been really rewarding and
wonderful to watch.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.579
And all of them now have
crossed the $1 million mark.

00:15:01.579 --> 00:15:05.270
And I do a lot of work with
Grameen America, helping

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:09.490
female entrepreneurs living below the
poverty line here in the United States.

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:14.510
One woman comes to mind, Brittany
Underwood, who started Akola Jewelry.

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:20.915
And I mentored her a little bit as well
in addition to helping to support her,

00:15:20.915 --> 00:15:26.936
and mentioned that she should try to
sell her product at Neiman Marcus.

00:15:26.936 --> 00:15:30.192
And a result,
Akola Jewelry is at all the Neimans.

00:15:30.192 --> 00:15:34.937
And she's doing a fabulous job giving
women, particularly in Dallas,

00:15:34.937 --> 00:15:36.897
homeless women employment.

00:15:36.897 --> 00:15:39.955
And her company started in Uganda.

00:15:39.955 --> 00:15:44.890
But the reality is people have
been asking me for 18 years,

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:50.480
multiple times a day, for 10 minutes of
my time to mentor them or to help them.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:56.360
And so,
I'm Working on a digital format for that.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:58.360
And it's in beta right now.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:01.340
And it's just a way to
answer how did I do this.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:05.590
And the real answer of how I did this is
Spanx started way before I cut the feet

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:06.960
out of my pantyhose.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.930
That's the sound bite in the press
that everybody's talked about for

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:11.340
almost 20 years.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:15.755
But it actually started
fundamentally when I was 16.

00:16:15.755 --> 00:16:18.010
I worked on mindset for myself.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:21.330
And I'm such a believer in
mindset is almost everything.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:24.060
And it just happened to be
a set of circumstances.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.670
I was riding my bike with one
of my best friends at 16, and

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:30.230
she was run over by a car and
killed in front of me.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:33.270
And then a few months
later my dad left home and

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:35.900
my parents separated and
ultimately got divorced.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:39.090
And when my dad moved out,
he came into my bedroom and

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:44.250
he handed me a cassette tape series called
How to be a No-Limit Person by Wayne Dyer.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:45.370
And he said,

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:50.040
sweetie, I wish I discovered this when I
was your age instead of the age of 40.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:50.810
And then he moved out.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:55.200
And so I started listening to
this How to be a No-Limit Person,

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:59.840
which was talking about visualization, law
of attraction, not caring about what other

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:05.420
people think about you,
not being consumed by the fear of failure.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:10.030
And just the clouds parted for me and
I thought I've spent a lot of time being

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:13.010
taught what to think, but
no one's really teaching me how to think.

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:17.130
And at 16, that was so
incredibly important.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:21.270
So in this digital platform, it's kind
of passing on a lot of these insights

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:24.990
that deal with mindset,
that I think is the real reason that Spanx

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:27.010
exists.
&gt;&gt; So,

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:29.390
you had this incredible
minset at the age of 16.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:34.730
And you're kind of almost paying it
forward by empowering women around you.

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:37.680
What role to you hope the Sarah Blakeley
Foundation will play in the empowerment

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:41.110
of women?
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean, my ultimate goal is that

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:44.085
the male and female energy on
the planet becomes balanced.

00:17:44.085 --> 00:17:50.972
[LAUGH] So I feel like, that was a snap.

00:17:50.972 --> 00:17:53.031
Was that a snap, okay?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:53.031 --> 00:17:54.530
&gt;&gt; All right, well, good,

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:56.583
that one person who agrees with me.

00:17:56.583 --> 00:17:57.083
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:57.083 --> 00:17:59.771
&gt;&gt; Yes. [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Where are you?

00:17:59.771 --> 00:18:01.193
Yeah, anyway, the male and

00:18:01.193 --> 00:18:04.571
female energy on the planet more balanced.
And the reason I say energy,

00:18:04.571 --> 00:18:06.881
I mentioned earlier is that
we all have the male and

00:18:06.881 --> 00:18:08.661
the female energy inside of us.
And

00:18:08.661 --> 00:18:12.810
I feel like the feminine has been pretty
squashed for long time on our planet.

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:14.810
And it's not serving the greater whole.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:18.710
So what can I do in my short
lifetime to elevate the feminine?

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:22.715
And that's what
the foundation is focused on.

00:18:22.715 --> 00:18:26.360
And how can I help the elevating and
supporting that,

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:31.920
and I take my opportunity as a woman
in this country so seriously.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:36.283
And I think part of the reason that I
do is because I got to watch my mom and

00:18:36.283 --> 00:18:41.660
my grandmothers and their lack of options,
and that was really hard.

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:46.260
And so we've been on the planet for
thousands of years or

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:49.440
millions of years depending on
who you ask and what you think.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:54.670
But the bottom line is my mom is only
22 years older than me, and my mom had

00:18:54.670 --> 00:19:00.700
about three of four options only
afforded to her because she was a woman.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:02.810
So in the grand scheme of life and

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:06.210
how long we've been on earth,
I'm like I made it by 22 years.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:07.650
I'm like, what, you're kidding me.

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:12.870
So I feel like a lot of my courage,
and I'm scared of a lot of things.

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:14.300
I mean, I'm afraid to fly.

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:15.280
I'm afraid of heights.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:16.850
I've got all kinds of things.

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:21.000
But I feel like where I get my
courage is that knowing and

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:25.250
thinking about I'm a woman born in
the right place at the right time.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.287
I don't want to squander that.

00:19:27.287 --> 00:19:32.240
And I want to make the most of that.
&gt;&gt; You're definitely a family woman.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.050
We see that a lot.

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:35.790
Your family's featured
on your Instagram a lot.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:37.563
How has your relationship
with your mother and

00:19:37.563 --> 00:19:40.050
grandmother influenced you?
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:45.960
I think they influenced me, I mean,
I love my mom and my grandmother so much.

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:48.830
My mom is the most supportive person ever,
but

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:54.540
they influenced me more by what
they didn't do than what they did.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:58.770
So my mom's an artist.

00:19:58.770 --> 00:19:59.900
She's very sweet.

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:01.660
She's very soft spoken.

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:04.916
She has low confidence,
but is a lovely and

00:20:04.916 --> 00:20:09.363
probably the sweetest person
I've ever known in my life.

00:20:09.363 --> 00:20:12.270
And she was a wonderful mother.

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:15.190
So I have four children
under the age of eight.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:18.265
I have an eight-year-old boy,
twin boys that are three, and

00:20:18.265 --> 00:20:19.720
a two-year-old daughter.

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:23.410
So this is vacation for me.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:24.995
&gt;&gt; I'm so happy to be here.

00:20:24.995 --> 00:20:29.820
[LAUGH] I woke up in my hotel room and
I'm like wow,

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:34.237
cool.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:34.237 --> 00:20:36.764
&gt;&gt; We're happy to have you here [LAUGH].

00:20:36.764 --> 00:20:41.370
You mentioned that you have so many fears,
including the fear of heights.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:44.600
But you're known as a leader who
does not shy away from her fears.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.350
So yes, you are terrified of heights,

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:48.890
but you've climbed on top
of a hot air balloon.

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:49.680
And to be clear,

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:55.240
this is on top, not in the basket,
to drink tea with Sir Richard Branson.

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:57.940
What fuels your drive to face
your fears head on as a leader?

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:01.870
Honestly, the opportunity to be a woman.

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:05.716
I feel like when you're doing something
in life, or you're living your life for

00:21:05.716 --> 00:21:09.580
something beyond just yourself, you'll
get courage you didn't know you had.

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:13.726
And so I really feel like when I do
things, I'm doing this for women,

00:21:13.726 --> 00:21:15.899
I'm doing this for women.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:15.899 --> 00:21:18.160
&gt;&gt; And it just helps me.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.850
But that was a particularly
insane moment in my life.

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:25.110
I did a reality show with Sir Richard
Branson many, many years ago and

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.160
it was his take on The Apprentice.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:31.640
And instead of the business challenges all
taking place in one city like New York,

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:33.620
they took place around the world.

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:38.317
And instead of, and this was the fine
print I didn't totally read, instead of if

00:21:38.317 --> 00:21:42.809
you lost your business challenge,
instead of getting in the boardroom, and

00:21:42.809 --> 00:21:45.987
him firing you,
you had to do a world record breaking,

00:21:45.987 --> 00:21:49.926
death-defying stunt with Richard.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:49.926 --> 00:21:52.177
&gt;&gt; Which I should have known because there

00:21:52.177 --> 00:21:56.920
was a 27-page contract that came over
from Fox that I was supposed to sign.

00:21:56.920 --> 00:22:00.780
And I emailed it to my dad who
I mentioned is a litigator.

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:04.650
And the contract basically was like,
we can submerge you under water.

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:06.480
We can put you in political unrest.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:08.760
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We can light you on fire, I mean,

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:09.727
it was crazy.

00:22:09.727 --> 00:22:13.923
And I emailed it to my dad and I said dad,
can you help me kind of tweak this and

00:22:13.923 --> 00:22:15.158
give me some advice?

00:22:15.158 --> 00:22:18.620
And all he wrote back was no
sane person would sign this,

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:19.437
love, Dad.

00:22:19.437 --> 00:22:22.369
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:22:22.369 --> 00:22:22.989
I signed it.

00:22:22.989 --> 00:22:24.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:26.613
&gt;&gt; And I spent two months with

00:22:26.613 --> 00:22:28.049
Richard Branson, and

00:22:28.049 --> 00:22:33.127
I just trusted my intuition that he wasn't
going to portray me in a way, with Fox.

00:22:33.127 --> 00:22:36.043
because, literally, my lawyer was on
his hands and knees like don't do this,

00:22:36.043 --> 00:22:38.240
you're four years into Spanx,
why would you ever put.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:43.372
But the first day of filming,
at 3 in the morning they woke us all up,

00:22:43.372 --> 00:22:47.354
and they said,
we're going to go out into the field by

00:22:47.354 --> 00:22:51.430
Richard Branson's home in Oxford, England.

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:53.566
And they handed me a helmet.

00:22:53.566 --> 00:22:56.088
And I was like, why do I need a helmet?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:22:56.088 --> 00:22:57.613
&gt;&gt; I'm an entrepreneur.

00:22:57.613 --> 00:22:59.120
And they were like, here's your helmet.

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:03.170
And I got in a hot air balloon and two hot
air balloons went up into the air over

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:06.240
the English countryside
first thing in the morning.

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:11.750
And it was at about 9,000, 10,000 feet,
and there was a balance beam connecting

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:14.710
the two hot air balloons and you were
asked to walk across the balance beam.

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:19.738
And I was one of only two
people who couldn't do it.

00:23:19.738 --> 00:23:20.913
Where did the people come from?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:20.913 --> 00:23:23.015
&gt;&gt; And so anyways, so I was like I can't.

00:23:23.015 --> 00:23:26.746
And then when they disconnected the two
hot air balloons, and Richard turned to me

00:23:26.746 --> 00:23:30.585
and he said, so, Sara, because you and
Tim couldn't get across the balance beam,

00:23:30.585 --> 00:23:33.190
now you have to do
something twice as hard.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:37.818
And I had to climb on a dangling rope
ladder the whole circumference of the hot

00:23:37.818 --> 00:23:42.892
air balloon at 10,000 feet in the air
going across the English countryside.

00:23:42.892 --> 00:23:44.432
And meet him on top of

00:23:44.432 --> 00:23:46.068
the balloon for tea.

00:23:46.068 --> 00:23:47.913
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sounds fun.

00:23:47.913 --> 00:23:49.313
&gt;&gt; So it took me 48

00:23:49.313 --> 00:23:53.813
minutes to climb the balloon,
it was insanely hard.

00:23:53.813 --> 00:23:58.570
And Yeah, so

00:23:58.570 --> 00:24:02.070
my friend Leslie gave this to me last
night over dinner and it's so cool.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:04.750
It's a hot air balloon with
someone dangling off of it.

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:08.560
[LAUGH] She was like,
I saw that and thought of you.

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:10.040
I'm like, thank you very much.

00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:13.560
It will remind me of
the courage it took me or

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:17.600
the insanity to do that.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:20.560
Since that show Richard Branson has become

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:21.490
a mentor to you.

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:24.340
What does that relationship and
that mentorship mean to you and

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:27.040
what does mentorship mean to
you more generally as well.

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:29.080
&gt;&gt; Well, he actually became,

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.280
he's a friend, he's a great friend and

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:36.440
what I like about Richard is he
has an incredible bias for action.

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:41.030
He is someone who just goes and
you just marvel at it.

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:44.230
He requires very little sleep,
he's always got a notebook with him and

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:46.350
he's always writing ideas in it.

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:47.560
He's a great delegator,

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.780
he doesn't ask anybody to do
anything he won't do himself.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:51.870
And he's funny.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:53.960
I mean he won't talk about a prankster.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.890
He is funny like he likes to prank people.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:58.560
So you just have to always be
prepared when you're around him

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:01.134
that something is going to jump out
&gt;&gt; Always have your

00:25:01.134 --> 00:25:01.900
helmet.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:06.870
Always have your helmet exactly.
&gt;&gt; So speaking of humor,

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.800
as you know here' at the JSB,
we have a class of humor and

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:14.430
business, we're Spanx models.
&gt;&gt; I think Spanx is so great.

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:15.120
I do.

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:16.260
I think it's so important.

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:20.200
Yeah, it's really important
when we're in business.

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:21.708
&gt;&gt; So tell me, why is that important.

00:25:21.708 --> 00:25:25.860
I know that as part of the Spanx training
camp, one of the mandatory learning

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:28.670
modules is comedy,
that every employee has to go through.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:30.870
So, why is that important?
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:35.210
Well, I just fond out in the green room
that I am one of only two CEO's that

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:38.140
you guys are aware of that
has also done standup comedy.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:41.450
And the other one is who started Twitter,
right?

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:42.008
Yep, so,

00:25:42.008 --> 00:25:46.170
I guess that would make me the only female
CEO that's ever done standup comedy.

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:52.080
Well, part of also wanting to stay very
connected to the feminine principles and

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.560
the feminine style of
leadership starting Spanx,

00:25:54.560 --> 00:26:01.280
I also felt like I didn't understand
why everybody was so serious.

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:02.640
So I thought, you know what?

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:07.400
I don't subscribe to the fact that you
have to act serious to be taken seriously.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:11.110
And I like to laugh at myself.

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:13.980
When I worked in corporate America for
a long time and everybody was super

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:17.090
uptight and super serious, there was
no humor, there was no levity and

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:20.245
then everybody at like five o'clock
became rangingly hilarious.

00:26:20.245 --> 00:26:21.141
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:21.141 --> 00:26:24.086
&gt;&gt; What's going on, like you know and so

00:26:24.086 --> 00:26:27.040
the alcohol probably helped at five.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:34.150
But anyway, I wanted to use humor and
I love to do that in the workplace.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:38.160
Yes, we have a boot camp at Spanx, a
training boot camp when you come on board

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:41.080
and one of the modules is
doing stand-up comedy and

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:45.450
I've used the comedic stuff
that I did in my marketing.

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:48.620
I mean I wrote don't worry we've got
your butt covered right on my package

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:52.480
when I first started in 2,000,
which was not very common to do.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:55.200
Especially taking it to
Neman Marcus to sell it.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:27:00.150
I named my company Spanx,
which made people laugh and

00:27:00.150 --> 00:27:03.730
believe it or not it was very shocking at
the time that I named the company Spanx,

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:06.140
I actually had people hang up on me often.

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:09.290
I would call them and say,
hi, I'm Sara from Spanx and

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:10.370
then they'd hang up.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:11.300
&gt;&gt; And I'd call back and

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:14.692
I'd go, I'm serious, I'm a real
company and my company's called Spanx.

00:27:14.692 --> 00:27:15.357
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:27:15.357 --> 00:27:16.395
&gt;&gt; And they'd go,

00:27:16.395 --> 00:27:18.870
I thought you were prank calling me.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:22.430
So and then naming my products.

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:24.580
I broke into the world's
most boring category.

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:25.840
I mean can we talk about shape wear?

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:27.110
Who wants to talk about shape wear and

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:30.720
undergarments but it was,
I named it power panties.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:34.090
I started naming all my products and
it made people laugh and it gave so

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:36.950
much energy and then all of
a sudden you Gwyneth Paltrow and

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:40.750
Julia Roberts flashing their spanx on red
carpets and saying, I'm wearing spanx.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:42.360
Like all these celebrities.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:44.780
And I think it is because
I chose to do humour and

00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:46.480
people wanted to participate in that.

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:48.810
There was energy around that.

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:54.900
So, I use it in every
aspect of the marketing.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.050
I use humor to turn around
a situation when I'm bombing it.

00:27:58.050 --> 00:27:59.670
It might give me a second chance.

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:02.590
When I cold-called to sell
fax machines door-to-door for

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:05.650
seven years, I learned very quickly that
if could make somebody laugh or smile,

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:08.590
I'd get another 30 seconds before
they'd slam the door in my face.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:13.080
There's just,
it helps you move through pain,

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:17.680
it's a wonderful tool.
&gt;&gt; What impact have you seen humor have

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:22.510
on your Spanx employees?
&gt;&gt; Well they are not as afraid to fail,

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:24.020
which is a really criritical lessson.

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:25.250
And I think companies,

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:29.660
if you can create a culture where they are
not terrified to fail or make a mistake,

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:35.490
then you're going to be highly
productive and more innovative culture.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:38.580
So, having people feel free
to laugh at themselves and

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:41.230
watching me as their
leader laughed at myself.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:46.290
I have ups meetings at Spanx, where we get
up and I tell them what I messed up at or

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:49.810
mistake that I made and
I usually tell funny story about it and

00:28:49.810 --> 00:28:52.240
I encourage everybody else in
the company they stand up and

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:55.900
say that and then share and
make it a funny story.

00:28:55.900 --> 00:29:00.970
And so, I think that helps.
&gt;&gt; Do you have any stand up comedian role

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:06.260
models?
&gt;&gt; I like Tina Fey a lot.

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:08.930
I think she is fabulous and
super funny and

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:12.900
I didn't grow up watching comedy or
stand up comedians.

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.800
I told you I had a crush on Gene Wilder.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:17.210
I had a crush on Gene Wilder
when I was little and

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:20.610
I tried to join the Gene Wilder
fan club and there wasn't one.

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:24.660
All my friends were like had crushes on
Andy Gibb and all these other fan clubs.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:29.650
And I looked up the Gene, my mom tried
to find a Gene Wilder fan club and

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:31.330
apparently I was the only
one trying to join that.

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:37.210
But I think he's so fabulous and
funny and very sexy.

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:39.680
My husband actually looks
a lot like Gene Wilder.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:41.227
That crazy curly hair going in every

00:29:41.227 --> 00:29:46.230
direction.
&gt;&gt; What lessons did you take away

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:51.660
from your time as a stand-up comedian?
&gt;&gt; What lessons did I take?

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:55.590
I mean, well,
one thing that I learned that was random.

00:29:55.590 --> 00:29:57.735
This isn't really a takeaway but
like The Green Room?

00:29:57.735 --> 00:29:59.830
I'm just like, why is it called
The Green Room, it's not green?

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:00.385
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:00.385 --> 00:30:01.655
&gt;&gt; The Green Room is never green.

00:30:01.655 --> 00:30:05.075
And then after about, six months of doing
stand up comedy, everyone sitting in

00:30:05.075 --> 00:30:08.345
the room was either throwing up or
about to throw up, and I'm like

00:30:08.345 --> 00:30:10.325
that's why it's called The Green Room.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:10.325 --> 00:30:11.525
&gt;&gt; Like, we're all just sitting there

00:30:11.525 --> 00:30:12.943
green.
&gt;&gt; No.

00:30:12.943 --> 00:30:17.060
&gt;&gt; But I mean,

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:19.830
I learned because I wrote all my
own comedy, so I have notebooks and

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.760
notebooks full of comedy, so
I learned the importance of one word,

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:27.140
I learned the importance of a comma,
I learned the importance of timing.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:30.749
I mean you could tell a joke and
the next night tell a joke and

00:30:30.749 --> 00:30:32.158
change one word in it.

00:30:32.158 --> 00:30:34.887
It's like a complete different reaction,
it's flat.

00:30:34.887 --> 00:30:40.610
So, there's so much importance in
the deliver and in how you write comedy.

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:45.330
I learned so much.

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:49.380
I learned that I needed to go invent
something because I actually wasn't

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:51.985
that funny to create a full
time career out of it.

00:30:51.985 --> 00:30:55.750
[LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; But as an entrepreneur,

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.530
I have seen you kind of pull
threads of comedy as you mentioned,

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:02.340
you've said that you tried to go out of
your way to embarass yourself on a regular

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:06.910
basis, why is that?
&gt;&gt; Well, I am curious about

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:11.380
the things that hold power of us and one
of the big fears is fear of embarassment.

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:13.780
We all have that, we have fear of failure.

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:18.600
So I'm always working on these things that
I feel like might hold me back and so

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:22.660
if I embarrass myself,
then it loses its power over me.

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:26.760
And especially if embarrassing
myself at times becomes the goal.

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:30.790
Then it feel like I don't know,

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:34.760
it's like I'm playing my own head
games with myself on that but

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:40.980
one example of embarrassing myself is I
joined Instagram and I joined, I feel

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:44.770
like I might be the last one on the planet
to have join it, it was about a year ago.

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:47.500
And my team is like,
join the Instagram, you got to do it,

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:48.580
you got to put yourself out there.

00:31:48.580 --> 00:31:53.097
And I was nervous and I wasn't sure
like I don't know The day that I joined,

00:31:53.097 --> 00:31:56.350
I happened to be flying to
New York on business, and

00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:58.256
I was at the Atlanta airport.

00:31:58.256 --> 00:32:01.472
And so I was like,
I'm going to go around the airport and

00:32:01.472 --> 00:32:04.530
personally ask people to follow me.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:06.830
&gt;&gt; And it was so embarrassing, and

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:08.160
I was mortified.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:10.770
And I was embarrassing myself and
I posted the whole video,

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:13.040
it's like my first post on Instagram.

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:14.820
It's me, because I'm like,
how does this work?

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:15.740
How do you get followers?

00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:20.940
So I was around Jackson Hartsfield
airport, and I even stood up in the video,

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.460
I'm filming myself, and
I make an announcement at my gate.

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:26.700
I'm like, excuse me, I'm Sara Blakely and

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:29.170
I just joined Instagram today,
will you follow me?

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:32.228
And literally [LAUGH] I go like this,
not one person looks up, they're like.

00:32:32.228 --> 00:32:33.287
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:32:33.287 --> 00:32:34.812
&gt;&gt; I was like, thank you,

00:32:34.812 --> 00:32:40.456
thank you.
&gt;&gt; I was going to ask if it worked,

00:32:40.456 --> 00:32:42.003
but-.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Not exactly.

00:32:42.003 --> 00:32:42.986
&gt;&gt; because I need more followers,

00:32:42.986 --> 00:32:44.151
that's why I'm asking [LAUGH].

00:32:44.151 --> 00:32:47.827
So you've given us many examples of how
you've been able to push yourself to face

00:32:47.827 --> 00:32:49.050
your fears.

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:51.125
What fears have you not
figured out how to face yet?

00:33:10.176 --> 00:33:11.700
&gt;&gt; I mean, I can't really think of one.

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:15.980
I work hard at them, and

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:20.770
sometimes my fear of heights and flying is
winning, and sometimes I'm winning at it.

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:25.920
I've fear of public speaker, sometimes
I'm winning sometimes I'm not at that.

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:30.806
There's just a lot of different things,
but, There's nothing

00:33:30.806 --> 00:33:35.743
that I feel like I'm not, Trying and

00:33:35.743 --> 00:33:40.424
working at, so.
&gt;&gt; So

00:33:40.424 --> 00:33:43.977
Sara, you talked about your
Instagram account, where you share

00:33:43.977 --> 00:33:49.460
literally unfiltered moments, as a mom,
as a wife, as a friend, and as a leader.

00:33:49.460 --> 00:33:52.050
What keeps you so
unbelievable grounded amid

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:56.040
all your outstanding success?
&gt;&gt; I

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:59.095
feel like success just makes you
more of who you already were.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:03.055
I feel like money and
success just does that, so

00:34:03.055 --> 00:34:07.450
it's like it holds a magnifying
glass up to who you are.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:10.650
People always ask me that, and
I am like, I don't know who else to be,

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:11.710
I am who I am.

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:17.040
So the money, the success, and so
I like to say if you were a jerk before

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:21.900
you got really successful, and a lot of
money, you become a bigger jerk, really.

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:24.750
And if you were insecure, you'll become
more insecure, if you were nice,

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:25.350
you become nicer.

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:27.110
If you were generous,
you become become more generous,

00:34:27.110 --> 00:34:30.660
it's literally just a magnifying glass.

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:36.960
But yeah, People ask me that,
I don't know any other way to be.

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:39.070
Just going through it,

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:41.760
figuring it out along the way.
&gt;&gt; Awesome, well,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:45.880
I know there's a couple of people in
the audience who have questions for you.

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:48.760
So I'm going to hand it over for a minute.
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:50.068
&gt;&gt; So if you have a question,

00:34:50.068 --> 00:34:51.495
please raise your hand, and

00:34:51.495 --> 00:34:59.940
we'll get a mic to you.
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:00.440
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:01.471
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for

00:35:01.471 --> 00:35:02.367
being here.
My name's Beth.

00:35:02.367 --> 00:35:03.153
&gt;&gt; Hi, Beth.

00:35:03.153 --> 00:35:05.011
&gt;&gt; And I'm in my second year, and

00:35:05.011 --> 00:35:08.022
about to have to leave this place.
The question

00:35:08.022 --> 00:35:11.432
that I've been wondering about that
you've spoken a little bit is I mean,

00:35:11.432 --> 00:35:14.559
your Instagram is just awesome.
There are not other CEOs who I want to

00:35:14.559 --> 00:35:17.652
spend time seeing what they're
up to in their daily life.

00:35:17.652 --> 00:35:20.522
And I'm wondering, what you think other

00:35:20.522 --> 00:35:25.606
people who are running companies and
business can learn from how you think

00:35:25.606 --> 00:35:31.430
about communication through social media?
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:35:31.430 --> 00:35:36.620
I haven't thought that much about what
other people are and aren't doing.

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:42.910
I love the idea of leaders and
CEOs showing vulnerabilities, and

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:50.290
showing the ups and downs, and I just
don't really subscribe in the feeling

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:54.540
that I need to put on any kind of a facade
to be taken seriously as a leader.

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:58.600
And so it's like this is my life,
this is who I am.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:02.660
I'm a mom of four, some days I'm
working it out, some days I'm not.

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:05.890
And I think that's important.

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:09.420
I also sort of have my own filter for
Instagram,

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:13.250
it's not planned out, I do it 100% myself.

00:36:13.250 --> 00:36:17.150
And I'm having fun with it, if I wake
up one day and it's not fun anymore,

00:36:17.150 --> 00:36:18.740
then I'll figure out a plan B.

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:22.655
But so
everything kind of happens organically and

00:36:22.655 --> 00:36:25.960
in the moment for my Instagram.

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:29.210
And I tend to think before I do it or

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:33.280
if it's happening, is this going to
inspire someone or make them feel better?.

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:41.217
I'm kind of interested in making other
people feel like they're not in it alone,

00:36:41.217 --> 00:36:45.147
or it's not the projection
of a perfect life.

00:36:52.138 --> 00:36:53.247
&gt;&gt; Much for being here, we're so

00:36:53.247 --> 00:36:54.060
excited to have you.

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:57.310
I'm Britney, I'm an MBA two as well,
about to graduate.

00:36:57.310 --> 00:37:01.370
And so as Dean Levin mentioned,
you're famous and very much admired for

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:02.670
your hustle.

00:37:02.670 --> 00:37:05.520
I think the story of paying
your friends to go buy your

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:08.710
product from the shelves of Neiman Marcus
is one that many of us have heard.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:12.830
And I'm just curious kind of
what hustle means to you,

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:14.850
whether you think it can be learned.

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:17.300
And also whether it's possible
to take hustle too far.

00:37:18.605 --> 00:37:19.903
&gt;&gt; [SOUND], that's a good question,

00:37:19.903 --> 00:37:22.065
that's a few questions,
I'm going to try to hit them all.

00:37:22.065 --> 00:37:24.480
I've got mommy memory.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:37:24.480 --> 00:37:26.040
&gt;&gt; So I'm going to start with the first

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:29.450
one which was what do I think of hustle?

00:37:29.450 --> 00:37:34.180
I feel like hustle is the willingness
to work really, really hard, and

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:37.670
then also your willingness
to get the job done.

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:43.135
Like kind of navigating and
doing what it takes.

00:37:43.135 --> 00:37:48.445
And there's so

00:37:48.445 --> 00:37:52.565
much about my journey where I was like,
I am not going to let the outcome, or

00:37:52.565 --> 00:37:56.455
my success, be contingent on other people,

00:37:56.455 --> 00:38:00.800
as much as I can control it,
help it, navigate it, I'm going to.

00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:07.210
So yeah, in the beginning especially,
still now, obviously I'm hustling.

00:38:08.710 --> 00:38:11.640
Where I am now, and I'm running around
the airport asking people to follow

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:13.460
me on Instagram, you know?

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:16.440
I just feel like it's innate in me also,
to a degree.

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:22.800
But In the beginning,
there's a few examples that come to mind.

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:27.320
You mentioned that I called my friends and
asked them to go buy Spanx, and

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:33.330
I wrote everybody checks and sent them a
check because I needed to drive momentum.

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:36.050
And I had no money to advertise, so
I'm like, this product's going to sit on

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:39.870
the shelf, so I needed to get
people to go in and buy it.

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:44.070
I also, when I went into the stores,
I realized very quickly that

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:46.980
my product was in the sleepiest
part of the department store.

00:38:46.980 --> 00:38:49.840
It was back in the corner and
nobody was going there.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:56.390
So then I immediately went to Target and

00:38:56.390 --> 00:39:01.460
I bought envelope dividers that
you put on your home desk,

00:39:01.460 --> 00:39:04.900
and I ran around Neiman Marcus and
put them at every register.

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:07.795
And I put Spanx in them,
and then I walked away.

00:39:07.795 --> 00:39:10.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:13.560
&gt;&gt; Nieman's has impeccable visual rules

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:14.490
and regulations,

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:18.170
and I did that because I had to get
my product out of the department.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:21.860
And because I did that,
women in shoes started buying Spanx and

00:39:21.860 --> 00:39:23.610
women in contemporary.

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:26.600
And those decisions made
such a big difference.

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:30.100
And by the time somebody figured out
that nobody else had approved it,

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:33.275
because everybody thought
somebody else approved it.

00:39:33.275 --> 00:39:33.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:35.570
&gt;&gt; It was so successful that the head of

00:39:35.570 --> 00:39:39.000
Neiman's was like, whatever this
girl's doing, let her keep doing it.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:43.354
I mean, I have so many stories like
that of just what do I need to do, yeah.

00:39:53.982 --> 00:39:55.828
&gt;&gt; I am second year biophysics PhD

00:39:55.828 --> 00:39:59.506
student.
&gt;&gt; My question is what advice did you

00:39:59.506 --> 00:40:04.139
receive about getting Spanx off the ground
in the first few years, good or bad?

00:40:04.139 --> 00:40:08.279
And what approach did you take
to taking this mentorship or

00:40:08.279 --> 00:40:10.910
advice into consideration?

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:15.041
And how did you navigate meshing
that advice with what you wanted to

00:40:15.041 --> 00:40:18.596
do based off of your intuition and
plan for your company?

00:40:18.596 --> 00:40:19.375
&gt;&gt; So what advice did I

00:40:19.375 --> 00:40:20.786
get in the beginning of Spanx?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:20.786 --> 00:40:21.865
&gt;&gt; We have compound questions here at

00:40:21.865 --> 00:40:22.443
Stanford.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:22.443 --> 00:40:25.400
&gt;&gt; That's okay.

00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:28.015
What advice was I given in
the beginning of Spanx?

00:40:32.163 --> 00:40:37.145
I mean, I remember somebody saying
to me as I was delegating and

00:40:37.145 --> 00:40:41.281
growing and
getting bigger that they said, Sarah,

00:40:41.281 --> 00:40:48.000
if it's 80% as good as you could do it
yourself, let it go and be very happy.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:53.264
And that was so freeing for me, because I
think I was always critiquing in my mind.

00:40:53.264 --> 00:40:58.284
That wasn't 100% exactly the way and
I was just like that became

00:40:58.284 --> 00:41:04.197
a really important lesson for me in
growing the business, and letting go more.

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:09.999
No, it's so funny.

00:41:09.999 --> 00:41:13.310
I don't have a mentor and I didn't have,
I never took a business class.

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:17.882
And Wayne Dyer, the gentleman I mentioned,
he's no longer living.

00:41:17.882 --> 00:41:23.259
He died almost two years ago,
I think the inspirational motivational

00:41:23.259 --> 00:41:29.195
speaker was someone that I really kind
of taught me without ever knowing him.

00:41:29.195 --> 00:41:30.871
Then I'm sorry, you had two or
three more questions in there.

00:41:30.871 --> 00:41:34.206
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:41:34.206 --> 00:41:36.893
&gt;&gt; Did I answer them or okay,

00:41:36.893 --> 00:41:40.335
cool.
&gt;&gt; We'll take maybe one last question from

00:41:40.335 --> 00:41:47.038
the audience before.
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Mark.

00:41:47.038 --> 00:41:49.222
I'm an MBA one, so
I'm not graduating for a little bit.

00:41:49.222 --> 00:41:50.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:41:50.790 --> 00:41:51.492
&gt;&gt; Hi, Mark.

00:41:51.492 --> 00:41:52.660
&gt;&gt; Hey, quick question.

00:41:52.660 --> 00:41:55.444
You talked a lot about intuition
versus data and decision-making, and

00:41:55.444 --> 00:41:56.044
also failing.

00:41:56.044 --> 00:41:59.645
I'm curious if there's a situation where
the numbers would have lead you one way,

00:41:59.645 --> 00:42:02.011
your intuition lead you another and
it was a failure and

00:42:02.011 --> 00:42:03.470
kind of what you learned from it.

00:42:07.150 --> 00:42:09.969
An example of intuition not working or

00:42:09.969 --> 00:42:13.225
working?
&gt;&gt; I guess an example where your intuition

00:42:13.225 --> 00:42:23.540
maybe led was a mistake.
&gt;&gt; I cannot give one example, I can't.

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:27.990
I can give examples on the other end
of not listening to my intuition and

00:42:27.990 --> 00:42:34.430
it being a mistake, but there's been so
much along the way where I and

00:42:34.430 --> 00:42:40.000
it requires you being quiet and
being receptive and

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:43.360
listening to whatever
the knowing is inside of you.

00:42:44.610 --> 00:42:48.608
It requires that.
&gt;&gt; I'd love to take the final question if

00:42:48.608 --> 00:42:49.610
that's okay, Sarah.
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:42:50.610 --> 00:42:52.640
&gt;&gt; You've said, the harder you work,

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:56.130
the more the universe will believe how
serious you are about your goals and

00:42:56.130 --> 00:42:57.940
dreams and will then show up to help.

00:42:59.060 --> 00:43:03.783
How much of your success do you
attribute to your hard work versus

00:43:03.783 --> 00:43:08.001
luck handed over by the universe?
&gt;&gt; I think they're so related,

00:43:08.001 --> 00:43:10.630
I really feel like
they're one in the same.

00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:13.427
And I mentioned when I
first started Spanx,

00:43:13.427 --> 00:43:16.007
I was selling fax machines door to door.

00:43:16.007 --> 00:43:17.580
I had a particularly bad day.

00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:21.309
I literally did get escorted out of
buildings almost all day every day and

00:43:21.309 --> 00:43:23.955
people ripped up my business
card in my face often.

00:43:23.955 --> 00:43:26.486
And one day,
I just pull off the side of the road and

00:43:26.486 --> 00:43:28.410
I was like this is not my life.

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:30.076
I'm in the wrong movie.

00:43:30.076 --> 00:43:31.181
Call the director.

00:43:31.181 --> 00:43:32.122
Call the producer.

00:43:32.122 --> 00:43:36.526
Like cut, this is not happening and
I went home to my apartment and

00:43:36.526 --> 00:43:38.230
I wrote my strengths.

00:43:38.230 --> 00:43:39.352
I was like what am I good at?

00:43:39.352 --> 00:43:42.150
And pretty much, the only thing
in the good column was sales.

00:43:42.150 --> 00:43:46.365
And so I started asking myself,
what is it about sales that I'm good at?

00:43:46.365 --> 00:43:49.685
And I realized I like giving somebody
something or selling them something that

00:43:49.685 --> 00:43:52.730
could help them, or change their life or
make them feel good.

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:56.894
So I actually wrote in my journal
that night, I'm going to invent

00:43:56.894 --> 00:44:01.815
a product that I can sell to millions of
people that will make them feel good and

00:44:01.815 --> 00:44:05.243
then I specifically asked the universe for
the idea.

00:44:05.243 --> 00:44:08.710
And I said out loud in my apartment,
universe give me the idea.

00:44:10.130 --> 00:44:13.754
And two years later, I cut the feet out
of my panty hose to go to a party and

00:44:13.754 --> 00:44:16.549
I couldn't figure out what
to wear under white pants.

00:44:16.549 --> 00:44:20.053
And I did it only one time,
because I was so open and

00:44:20.053 --> 00:44:25.666
receptive to what the universe was going
to provide for me and it took two years.

00:44:25.666 --> 00:44:29.010
And I was always looking, but
it showed up and I was going to pursue it.

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:31.490
I wasn't sure if that was the idea or
not, so

00:44:31.490 --> 00:44:34.116
I've been in partnership
with the universe.

00:44:34.116 --> 00:44:38.307
I feel like the universe, my CFO who's

00:44:38.307 --> 00:44:43.262
a little bit more analytical
minded than I am.

00:44:43.262 --> 00:44:46.960
She now says, the universe is
the best employee on the payroll.

00:44:46.960 --> 00:44:50.000
Because they're free, it's free.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:52.029
&gt;&gt; So she's now a believer.

00:44:52.029 --> 00:44:57.203
And you know what's so funny is about
like when I first started Spanx too,

00:44:57.203 --> 00:45:00.622
I joined a group and
they put me in a forum of men and

00:45:00.622 --> 00:45:03.899
there were only men in
this entrepreneurial.

00:45:03.899 --> 00:45:07.847
It's like a YPO across the country and
they put me in my chapter in Atlanta and

00:45:07.847 --> 00:45:10.640
I got put in with ten guys,
I'm the only girl.

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:15.750
I have met with these 10 guys every
single month for 17 years now.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:17.148
They're like my brothers.

00:45:17.148 --> 00:45:19.747
But they will tell you
when I first started,

00:45:19.747 --> 00:45:23.892
they were all taking bets in the group
of how long I'd be in business and

00:45:23.892 --> 00:45:28.410
I would always say things like they go
around the room and talk about things.

00:45:28.410 --> 00:45:29.523
And I would say, well,

00:45:29.523 --> 00:45:31.313
I've asked the universe to do this.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:31.313 --> 00:45:32.946
&gt;&gt; And I taking care of this and

00:45:32.946 --> 00:45:37.644
I have visualized that I'm going to be
on Oprah, and I have, and they're like.

00:45:37.644 --> 00:45:38.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:41.780
&gt;&gt; And then my results kept going up and

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:42.910
up, and up.

00:45:42.910 --> 00:45:46.412
And then eventually after a couple of
years one by one and I'm not kidding,

00:45:46.412 --> 00:45:49.359
every single one of them pulled
me aside secretly and was like so

00:45:49.359 --> 00:45:53.671
how do you talk to the universe?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:53.671 --> 00:45:54.428
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:45:54.428 --> 00:45:55.111
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:45:55.111 --> 00:45:55.696
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:45:55.696 --> 00:45:58.733
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:45:58.733 --> 00:45:59.840
&gt;&gt; Thank you so

00:45:59.840 --> 00:46:00.556
much.
&gt;&gt; Thank you, everybody.

